        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_012swubiDpUZnG9hifpj7tyE",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022pages/single-composition-point.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Single Composition Point\\ndescription: How credential scoping and runtime path resolution work together through a single composition point to enforce isolation and reduce scattered configuration logic.\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Single-Composition-Point\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - tech/dotnet\\n  - architecture/composition\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] explicitly describe their implementation as a single, centralized decision point. The connection between them emerges from how they both reject distributed pattern-application in favor of a single enforcer.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2027-05-14\\n---\\n\\n[[credential-scoping|Credential Scoping]] and [[runtime-paths|Runtime Path Resolution]] are two applications of the same architectural principle: **make a critical decision in exactly one place, enforce it uniformly, and never scatter the logic across the codebase.**\\n\\n**Credential Scoping** ([[credential-scoping]]) centralizes the decision about which process gets the Anthropic API key: the Hub\u0027s secrets loader reads the key at startup and injects it only into child process environments \u2014 never into the Hub\u0027s own process, never inherited or written by downstream code. This prevents any other part of the system from independently deciding how to acquire or manage the credential.\\n\\n**Runtime Path Resolution** ([[runtime-paths]]) centralizes the decision about where every file-system location lives: a single path resolver at startup reads configuration in a fixed precedence order (command line \u2192 environment variables \u2192 config file \u2192 code defaults), validates all inputs, and publishes the resolved set. No other part of the codebase is allowed to re-discover locations by reading the working directory or environment directly.\\n\\nBoth decisions share the same force: they recognize that if the decision is scattered \u2014 if each component decides independently how to acquire the credential, or where to find a file \u2014 the system becomes harder to reason about, harder to test, and more vulnerable to inconsistencies and security lapses. By making the decision once and distributing only the result, they maintain:\\n\\n- **Predictability**: every component sees a consistent, already-validated state, not its own partially-resolved view.\\n- **Auditability**: the decision and all its inputs pass through a single, observable point.\\n- **Isolation**: components that don\u0027t need to know how the decision was made don\u0027t get to decide; they get a sealed, immutable result.\\n\\nThis pattern is not unique to credentials and paths \u2014 it\u0027s a general principle for any critical system-wide decision in Grimoire\u0027s architecture.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
