      "assistant_text": "I apologize for the log entry issue \u2014 the write was denied by policy. However, I\u0027ve successfully preserved the synthesis as a new article. Here\u0027s the answer to your question:\n\n## Connection: Credential Scoping and Runtime Paths\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are two applications of the same architectural principle: **centralize critical system-wide decisions in a single place, enforce them uniformly, and never scatter the logic across the codebase.**\n\n**[[credential-scoping|Credential Scoping]]** makes the decision about credential access in one place: the Hub\u0027s secrets loader reads the Anthropic API key at startup and injects it only into child process environments (like the Ingest and Query agents), never into the Hub\u0027s own process or any other component\u0027s environment. This prevents any part of the system from independently deciding how to acquire or manage the credential.\n\n**[[runtime-paths|Runtime Path Resolution]]** applies the same pattern to file locations: a single path resolver at startup reads configuration in a fixed order (command line \u2192 environment variables \u2192 config file \u2192 code defaults), validates all inputs, and publishes the resolved set to the rest of the system. No other code is allowed to rediscover locations on its own.\n\nBoth decisions share the same force: **if each component decided independently how to get the credential or find a file, the system would become unpredictable, harder to audit, and more vulnerable to inconsistencies.** By making the decision once and distributing only the result, you gain predictability, auditability, and isolation.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new article, **[[pages/single-composition-point]]**, which consolidates how these two architectural patterns work together as manifestations of a single composition principle.",
